{{- /*
capabilities.tmpl — render-time probes of MOMENTARY host state, for
transient-blocking skip sites only.

WHAT THIS IS FOR. A `transient-blocking` site (see skip.sh.tmpl) exits 0, so
chezmoi records it as a successful run. It can only re-run on its own if the
script's RENDERED CONTENT changes when the missing precondition appears. This
partial emits a two-valued token for exactly that purpose; the caller hashes it
through fingerprint.tmpl's `values` key, so the token flipping changes the
script's content and chezmoi re-runs it.

WHY A PROBE AND NOT A MARKER FILE. Measured on chezmoi v2.71.0 against a scratch
source and destination:

  marker whose content changes on each skip  -> script re-runs on EVERY apply
                                                (an always-run script)
  marker with stable content                 -> ran twice, then stalled
                                                permanently, and did NOT re-run
                                                when the precondition appeared
  render-time probe of the precondition      -> ran once, byte-stable across
                                                three further applies, re-ran
                                                immediately once the
                                                precondition appeared

Only the third is both stable while the precondition is absent and different
once it is present, which is what R6 and AE3 together require.

THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE FACT REGISTRY. `.chezmoidata/facts.yaml:5-9`
excludes momentary state — a live D-Bus session, a `sudo -n` credential cache —
from host IDENTITY, and that exclusion stands. A capability here:

  - never appears in a `gates:` expression and never gates anything,
  - is consumed only as a fingerprint input, so it can only ever change WHEN a
    script re-runs, never WHETHER it is allowed to act,
  - therefore cannot fail open, which is the property facts.yaml's per-fact
    `whenFalse:` doctrine exists to protect.

Adding a capability is not adding a fact. Do not add a `gates:` consumer for
one; if a decision needs it, that decision belongs in the fact registry with a
declared fail-safe direction.

EVERY PROBE MUST EXIT 0 AND EMIT ONE OF TWO FIXED TOKENS. A probe that can fail
the render, or that can emit a third value (a timestamp, a device id, a version
string), would either break every `chezmoi` command or turn its consumers into
always-run scripts. Both are worse than the bug this fixes.

COST, STATED PLAINLY — AND A HARD LIMIT ON HOW FAR THIS FORM SCALES. Each call
here runs one subprocess at RENDER time, which is once per chezmoi command, not
once per apply: `chezmoi status` and `chezmoi diff` pay it too. `sudo-usable`
additionally runs `sudo -n true`, so a host that logs failed sudo attempts sees
more of them than before. That is the price of the sudo consumers self-healing;
the measured alternative is that they never self-heal at all.

The site inventory found ~47 transient-blocking sites needing roughly 35
distinct probes. At that count this per-call form is the wrong shape: ~35
subprocesses on every chezmoi command. BEFORE converting sites in bulk, move
probe resolution into the `read-source-state.pre` hook that already writes
${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/chezmoi/facts.yaml, and have this partial read that
cache through a stat-guarded `include` exactly the way facts.tmpl does. That is
one subprocess per command regardless of probe count, and it makes rendering
reproducible within a command rather than per call. This partial's ARGUMENT
CONTRACT does not change when that happens — only where the value comes from —
so call sites written against it today stay correct.

Until that rework lands, keep the probe count small and prefer probes with no
side effect at all.

ARGUMENT — a dict:

  {{ includeTemplate "capabilities.tmpl" (dict "ctx" . "probe" "sudo-usable") }}

Emits `available` or `unavailable`, with no trailing newline, so it can be used
directly as a fingerprint value.
*/ -}}
{{- if not (kindIs "map" .) -}}
{{-   fail (printf "capabilities.tmpl: expected a dict, got a %s (%v). Call it as {{ includeTemplate \"capabilities.tmpl\" (dict \"ctx\" . \"probe\" \"sudo-usable\") }}" (kindOf .) .) -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- $probe := "" -}}{{- if hasKey . "probe" -}}{{- $probe = .probe -}}{{- end -}}
{{- $known := list "sudo-usable" "session-bus-present" -}}
{{- if not (has $probe $known) -}}
{{-   fail (printf "capabilities.tmpl: unknown probe %q. Known probes: %s. Add a new probe to this partial rather than inlining a command at the call site, so every render-time probe stays enumerable and reviewable in one place." $probe (join ", " $known)) -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- if ne .ctx.chezmoi.os "linux" -}}
unavailable
{{- else if eq $probe "sudo-usable" -}}
{{- /* Root needs no sudo. Otherwise a non-interactive check: succeeds on a
   cached credential or password-less sudo, fails closed on anything else.
   `|| true` and the timeout keep a hung or missing sudo from failing the
   render. */ -}}
{{- output "sh" "-c" "if [ \"$(id -u)\" = 0 ]; then printf available; elif timeout 5 sudo -n true >/dev/null 2>&1; then printf available; else printf unavailable; fi" | trim -}}
{{- else if eq $probe "session-bus-present" -}}
{{- /* Pure environment and socket test — no subprocess side effects. Mirrors
   the runtime condition its consumers already test. */ -}}
{{- output "sh" "-c" "if [ -n \"${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:-}\" ] || [ -S \"${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/nonexistent}/bus\" ]; then printf available; else printf unavailable; fi" | trim -}}
{{- end -}}
